    intro: "John Fowles's The Collector (1963) is a standalone novel told in two parts: first the account of Frederick Clegg, a lonely butterfly collector who wins the football pools and kidnaps the art student Miranda Grey; then Miranda's own diary, covering the same events from her perspective. One of the most disturbing psychological novels in English and a precise examination of what class resentment, inadequacy, and the collecting impulse look like taken to their logical extreme.",
    startWith: 'The Collector',
    books: [
      { title: 'The Collector', author: 'John Fowles', year: 1963, note: 'Frederick\'s account and Miranda\'s diary — complete standalone, two perspectives on the same captivity. Short, devastating, and impossible to put down.' },
      { title: 'The French Lieutenant\'s Woman', author: 'John Fowles', year: 1969, note: 'Charles Smithson falls in love with the mysterious Sarah Woodruff in Victorian Lyme Regis — Fowles\'s most celebrated novel; more formally experimental than The Collector.', isOptional: true },
      { title: 'The Magus', author: 'John Fowles', year: 1965, note: 'A young Englishman teaching on a Greek island encounters a mysterious millionaire who subjects him to an elaborate game of illusion — Fowles\'s most ambitious and most flawed novel; essential for the full Fowles.', isOptional: true },
      { title: 'Daniel Martin', author: 'John Fowles', year: 1977, note: 'A Hollywood screenwriter returns to England and confronts his past — Fowles\'s most autobiographical novel, quieter and longer than his earlier work.', isOptional: true },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'Why is Frederick Clegg so disturbing?', a: 'Frederick is disturbing because his crimes emerge so clearly from recognizable and even sympathetic sources: loneliness, inadequacy, class resentment, and a kind of love that is incapable of recognizing the other person as a subject rather than an object. The butterfly metaphor that runs through the novel is the key: Frederick collects butterflies by killing them and mounting them, and he brings exactly the same logic to Miranda — he wants to possess her, which means he must pin her down; he cannot conceive of any form of relationship that doesn\'t involve his complete control over the object of his desire. Fowles makes Frederick\'s logic legible and even comprehensible without making it sympathetic, which is the most disturbing achievement in the novel.' },
      { q: 'What is Miranda\'s diary meant to show?', a: 'The second section, Miranda\'s diary covering the same period from her perspective, does several things simultaneously. It gives the reader information that Frederick\'s account has suppressed or distorted — his self-serving memory cannot be trusted, and Miranda\'s account reveals the full horror of what he is doing in ways his account minimizes. It also shows Miranda\'s moral and intellectual development during captivity: she is forced to examine her own attitudes toward class (she had been dismissive of people like Frederick in her previous life), her artistic development, and her relationship to death. The diary is also structurally important: by giving Miranda a voice, Fowles insists on her subjectivity against Frederick\'s attempt to reduce her to an object.' },
      { q: 'Is The Collector autobiographical?', a: 'Not in any literal sense. Fowles was not a kidnapper; but he was deeply interested in the psychology of male inadequacy and its relationship to art and collecting, and The Collector engages with themes that run through all his work: the relationship between the collector-observer and the living object they want to possess, the class dynamics of British life, and the moral failure of a certain kind of aesthetic appreciation that reduces the living to the inanimate. Fowles has said that both Frederick and Miranda represent aspects of the same psychological dynamic that interested him throughout his career.' },
    ],
