    slug: 'rebecca',
    intro: "Daphne du Maurier's Rebecca (1938) is a standalone Gothic novel of suspense, narrated by a shy, unnamed young woman who marries the wealthy widower Maxim de Winter and moves to his magnificent Cornish estate, Manderley — only to find herself haunted by the perfect ghost of his first wife, Rebecca. One of the bestselling novels of the twentieth century.",
    startWith: 'Rebecca',
    books: [
      { title: 'Rebecca', author: 'Daphne du Maurier', year: 1938, note: 'The complete novel — Manderley, Mrs. Danvers, the shadow of Rebecca. Stands entirely alone.' },
      { title: 'My Cousin Rachel', author: 'Daphne du Maurier', year: 1951, note: 'The closest thematic companion to Rebecca — a young man inherits an estate and becomes obsessed with the mysterious widow his guardian married; equally gripping, equally ambiguous.', isOptional: true },
      { title: 'Jamaica Inn', author: 'Daphne du Maurier', year: 1936, note: 'Du Maurier\'s atmospheric Cornish Gothic — a young woman arrives at her aunt\'s inn on the bleak Cornish moors and discovers it is at the center of a wrecking conspiracy. Excellent for Rebecca readers who want more du Maurier Cornwall.', isOptional: true },
      { title: 'The Birds and Other Stories', author: 'Daphne du Maurier', year: 1952, note: 'Du Maurier\'s short fiction at its most unsettling — the title story was the basis for Hitchcock\'s film. Shows the range of her Gothic imagination in compressed form.', isOptional: true },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'Is Rebecca based on a true story?', a: 'Rebecca is not based on a true story, though du Maurier drew on personal experience and real places. The fictional Manderley was inspired by Menabilly, a real estate in Cornwall that du Maurier later rented and lived in for many years. The psychological dynamic of the novel — a second wife overshadowed by an idealized first — reflected anxieties du Maurier described in her own life, including her feelings about her husband\'s first love. The character of Rebecca herself and the plot are entirely fictional. Du Maurier was accused of plagiarism by the estate of Brazilian writer Carolina Nabuco, whose novel A Sucessora had a similar premise; the case was settled out of court. Du Maurier denied having read the book, and most literary scholars regard the similarities as coincidental or superficial.' },
      { q: 'Who is narrating Rebecca, and why is she never named?', a: 'The narrator of Rebecca is Maxim de Winter\'s second wife, whose given name is never revealed in the novel. Du Maurier\'s choice was deliberate: the namelessness emphasizes the narrator\'s lack of identity and self-assurance, her sense of being a nonentity compared to the vivid, fully realized Rebecca who preceded her. By contrast, Rebecca has a name, a personality, a reputation, a presence that fills every room of Manderley even after her death. The narrator\'s namelessness is also the reader\'s namelessness — we enter the novel as she does, uncertain and overshadowed. Du Maurier maintained in interviews that she had simply never thought of a name and found the omission worked so well she kept it.' },
      { q: 'Is Rebecca a romance or a mystery?', a: 'Rebecca defies easy genre categorization, which is part of what makes it a perennial classic. It has the bones of a Gothic romance — brooding hero, isolated estate, atmospheric dread, a dark secret — and it is often shelved with romance fiction. But it is also a psychological thriller in the tradition of Wilkie Collins, structured around a revelation that recontextualizes everything that came before. The mystery of Rebecca\'s death and the truth about her character drive the novel\'s second half. Du Maurier herself considered it primarily a study in jealousy and self-doubt, and the final revelation is as much a moral and psychological turning point as a plot twist. Modern readers often find it closer to literary suspense than to conventional romance.' },
    ],
