    intro: "Anthony Trollope's The Way We Live Now (1875) is the largest and most ambitious of his novels — a satire of financial speculation and social corruption in 1870s England organized around the rise and fall of Augustus Melmotte, the fraudulent financier whose social ascent exposes the moral bankruptcy of every class of English society. Written in nine months after Trollope's return from Australia and regarded by many critics as the greatest Victorian novel about money and its power to corrupt everything it touches.",
