    slug: 'between-the-world-and-me',
    intro: "Ta-Nehisi Coates's Between the World and Me (2015) is a letter to his teenage son, written in the tradition of James Baldwin's The Fire Next Time, about what it means to possess a Black body in the United States — its vulnerability to police violence, its history of destruction and exploitation, and the question of how to live meaningfully in spite of this knowledge. Winner of the National Book Award for Nonfiction and one of the defining works of American political writing in the twenty-first century.",
    startWith: 'Between the World and Me',
    books: [
      { title: 'Between the World and Me', author: 'Ta-Nehisi Coates', year: 2015, note: "The complete standalone work; reads in about two hours. The essential starting point for Coates's nonfiction." },
      { title: 'We Were Eight Years in Power', author: 'Ta-Nehisi Coates', year: 2017, note: "A collection of Coates's eight essays for The Atlantic (one per year of the Obama presidency) with new introductions situating each piece in his evolving thinking — the natural companion volume to Between the World and Me.", isOptional: true },
    ],
    faq: [
      { q: 'What is Between the World and Me about?', a: "Between the World and Me is a letter from Coates to his fifteen-year-old son, Samori, about the vulnerability of the Black body in America — how American society, from its founding through chattel slavery to the contemporary prison system and police violence, has been organized around the exploitation and destruction of Black bodies. Coates writes from the position of someone who has studied this history rigorously (his journalism for The Atlantic is among the best historical writing about race in America) and who has seen it personally: the book's inciting occasion is the death of his college friend Prince Jones, killed by a plainclothes police officer in 2000. Coates does not offer consolation or optimism — he describes the America that has produced these conditions without flinching from what it is — and this refusal to comfort is both the book's most controversial feature and the one that gives it the force of genuine thought rather than reassurance." },
      { q: 'How does Between the World and Me relate to James Baldwin?', a: "Between the World and Me takes its title from a Richard Wright poem and its epistolary form and its moral seriousness from James Baldwin's The Fire Next Time (1963) — a letter from Baldwin to his nephew about what it means to be Black in America at the moment of the civil rights movement. Coates has described Baldwin as the writer who most shaped his understanding of what it was possible for an American writer to say, and the comparison between the two books is explicitly invited by the form: both are letters from a Black American man to a younger relative about the condition of Black life in America, written at a moment of racial crisis (Baldwin in 1963, Coates in 2015, in the immediate aftermath of Ferguson, Staten Island, and the deaths of Michael Brown and Eric Garner). The differences are as instructive as the similarities: Baldwin wrote in the register of prophecy and moral witness, while Coates writes in the register of historical materialism, and the two books illuminate each other across fifty years." },
      { q: 'Is Between the World and Me optimistic?', a: "No — and Coates has been criticized for this, particularly by those who felt that his refusal of hope left readers with no response to the conditions he describes. Coates argues that American racism is not an aberration or a failure to live up to American ideals but a feature of American society that is deeply embedded in its founding, its economy, and its culture, and that the 'Dream' of American life — the suburbs, the safety, the comfort — has been built on the destruction of Black bodies. He does not offer the narrative of progress toward justice that characterizes much liberal writing about race, and he is skeptical of the forms of spiritual consolation that sustained earlier generations of Black Americans, including Christianity. What he offers instead is rigorous attention — to history, to the body, to his son's actual life — and the example of his own sustained engagement with these difficult truths as a form of integrity, even without the comfort of hope." },
    ],
